 "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Andy Ogles (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Republicans enter the fall as clear favorites to hold Tennessee's 5th District, an open seat after Andy Ogles lost renomination. The district leans Republican by about ten points, and seven of nine major race ratings call it Safe or Solid R, with the other two at Likely R; markets give the Democrat roughly an eight percent chance. The only public poll, conducted in late August by a Democratic-affiliated firm, showed Charlie Hatcher ahead of Chaz Molder 48 to 40.\n\nMolder has run a well-funded campaign, raising about $2.6 million and holding a large cash advantage, while Democratic outside groups have spent against Hatcher. Headlines about a massage parlor investigation tied to candidates in the district add some noise to the contest. Still, the structural Republican lean, the ratings consensus, and the polling picture make an upset possible but unlikely.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.079, "margin": -8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0675, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0985, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -10.0}]}